---
import Layout from '../../studio/layouts/Layout.astro';
import { mailto, studio } from '../../apps/registry';

const title = 'Privacy Policy — Paperdot';
const description = 'Privacy policy for Paperdot (com.yanerapps.paperdot), a Bullet Journal app for Android by Yaner Apps.';
---

<Layout title={title} description={description}>
  <div class="prose">
    <nav aria-label="Paperdot navigation" class="product-nav">
      <a href="/privacy">Privacy by app</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> Paperdot
      <span> · </span><a href="/paperdot/support">Support</a>
    </nav>
    <div class="prose-header">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="lede">Paperdot for Android (<code>com.yanerapps.paperdot</code>)</p>
      <div class="meta">
        <span>Effective: from Paperdot&rsquo;s release on Google Play</span>
        <span>&middot;</span>
        <span>Updated: 30 Oct 2026</span>
      </div>
    </div>

    <p>
      This policy explains what Paperdot does with your data. Paperdot is made by Yaner Apps, an independent
      developer studio ("we", "us"). It applies to the Paperdot Android application and to this website.
    </p>

    <div class="card">
      <p><strong>In short:</strong> Paperdot has no user accounts and no servers of our own. Your journal stays
      on your device. Paperdot reads your calendar only to show events next to your logs, and never saves them.
      This version shows no ads. If you subscribe to Pro, Google Play processes the purchase and payment, and we
      never see your payment details. We never sell your personal information.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>1. Data that stays on your device</h2>
    <p>
      The following is stored in the app's private storage and is never transmitted to us &mdash; we operate
      no server and have no way to read it:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Your journal: entries, logs, collections, trackers and their history</li>
      <li>Reminders you set on entries</li>
      <li>App settings, including app lock, the calendars you chose to show and the backup reminder</li>
      <li>Your Pro status, as a copy signed with a key that stays in your device's secure keystore</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      You can delete entries, collections and trackers inside the app at any time. Uninstalling Paperdot
      removes everything stored on the device.
    </p>

    <h2>2. Permissions</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Permission</th>
            <th>What Paperdot does with it</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Calendar (read only, optional)</td>
            <td>Shows events from the calendars you choose next to your daily and monthly logs. Events are read when a page is shown and are never stored in your journal, backups, exports or search. Paperdot cannot create or change calendar events</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Notifications (optional)</td>
            <td>Shows the reminders you set and the backup reminder</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Run at startup</td>
            <td>Puts your reminders back after the device restarts</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Biometrics</td>
            <td>Lets app lock ask Android to confirm it is you, with your fingerprint, face or screen lock. Android checks it; Paperdot only learns whether it succeeded</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Network access</td>
            <td>Used only by Google Play Billing, to show Pro prices and confirm a subscription</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Paperdot does not ask for your location, contacts, camera, microphone or files. Text you share into
      Paperdot from another app becomes a journal entry on your device.
    </p>

    <h2>3. Data sent to third parties</h2>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Recipient</th>
            <th>What is sent</th>
            <th>Why</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Google Play Billing</td>
            <td>Which Pro plan you chose, your purchase, and an identifier the app derives on your device with a one-way hash</td>
            <td>To sell Pro, confirm that you have it, and help Google detect fraud</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Your journal, reminders, calendar events and settings are never sent to Google or to anyone else.
      This version of Paperdot shows no ads and does not start an advertising SDK, so it does not use your
      advertising ID. If a future version adds ads, this policy will be updated before it ships.
    </p>

    <h2>4. Subscribing to Pro</h2>
    <p>
      Paperdot Pro is an auto-renewing subscription sold and billed entirely by Google Play. Google Play takes
      the payment, and your payment method and billing details stay with Google. We never receive your card
      number, billing address or other payment information, and Paperdot has no server that receives your
      purchases. Google Play gives us order records for our sales in the Play Console, such as the order
      number, plan, price and date, which we use only for accounting, refunds and support.
    </p>
    <p>
      To unlock Pro, the app asks Google Play whether you have it and keeps a signed copy of the answer in
      private storage, so Pro keeps working for up to 7 days when you are offline. This copy is excluded from
      Android cloud backup and device-to-device transfer. You can cancel at any time in Google Play. When Pro
      ends, nothing in your journal is changed or deleted.
    </p>
    <p>
      Refunds and changes of payment method are handled by Google Play under your Google account. Google's
      handling of payment data is governed by the
      <a href="https://payments.google.com/payments/apis-secure/u/0/get_legal_document?ldo=0&amp;ldt=privacynotice" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google Payments Privacy Notice</a>.
    </p>

    <h2>5. Backups and exports</h2>
    <p>
      Paperdot can write files to places you choose: a full backup (<code>.paperdot.json</code>), a Markdown
      export and, with Pro, a PDF export. These files contain your journal in plain text. They are created only
      when you ask for them, they are not uploaded anywhere by Paperdot, and once they leave the app they are
      yours to protect. Sharing an export through Android's share sheet sends it to the app you pick.
    </p>
    <p>
      Android's own cloud backup may include your journal and settings if you have enabled it on your device;
      app lock, calendar choices and your Pro status are left out of it. That backup is managed by Google under
      your Google account, not by us.
    </p>

    <h2>6. Notifications and the widget</h2>
    <p>
      Reminders and the home screen widget can show the text of your entries, so anyone who can see your screen
      can see them. App lock hides Paperdot's preview in recent apps but does not hide notifications or the
      widget. You can hide sensitive notification content on the lock screen in Android's notification
      settings.
    </p>

    <h2>7. What we do not do</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>No user accounts, sign-in, or profiles</li>
      <li>No servers operated by us, and therefore no copy of your journal held by us</li>
      <li>No ads, analytics or crash-reporting SDKs started by this version</li>
      <li>No location permission and no use of your location</li>
      <li>No sale of personal information</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>8. Children</h2>
    <p>
      Paperdot is not directed to children under 13, and we do not knowingly collect data from children.
      Since the app collects nothing, a child's journal stays on their device like anyone else's.
    </p>

    <h2>9. Data retention and deletion</h2>
    <p>
      Because your data lives on your device, you control its lifetime: it stays until you delete it or
      uninstall the app. There is nothing for us to delete on our side, since we hold nothing. Data held by
      Google as a result of a purchase is subject to Google's own retention practices.
    </p>

    <h2>10. Security</h2>
    <p>
      Your journal is kept in the app's private storage, which other apps cannot read on a non-rooted device.
      App lock adds a check with your fingerprint, face or screen lock each time you come back to the app.
      Backup and export files are not encrypted, so keep them somewhere safe. The billing library talks to
      Google over HTTPS.
    </p>

    <h2>11. Additional information for the EEA, UK and Switzerland</h2>
    <p>
      This section gives the information required by the EU and UK General Data Protection Regulation and
      the Swiss Federal Act on Data Protection.
    </p>

    <h3>Controller</h3>
    <p>
      The controller for the processing described in this policy is Yaner Yang, publishing as Yaner Apps,
      reachable at <a href={mailto()}>{studio.email}</a>. Google acts as an independent
      controller for the data it receives, under its own policies.
    </p>

    <h3>Purposes and legal bases</h3>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Purpose</th>
            <th>Data</th>
            <th>Legal basis</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td>Providing Pro you subscribed to</td>
            <td>Your Google Play purchase status, the hashed identifier sent with a purchase, and the order records Google Play provides</td>
            <td>Necessary to perform the contract you entered into when subscribing (Art. 6(1)(b) GDPR)</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td>Answering your emails</td>
            <td>Your email address and your message</td>
            <td>Legitimate interests in responding to you (Art. 6(1)(f)); we delete the correspondence once the matter is closed, and within 12 months at the latest</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>
      Data that stays on your device (section 1) is not processed by us at all. You are not required to
      provide any personal data. We make no decisions based solely on automated processing.
    </p>

    <h3>International transfers</h3>
    <p>
      Google may process purchase data in the United States and other countries, relying on the EU-U.S. Data
      Privacy Framework, its UK Extension and the Swiss-U.S. Data Privacy Framework, and on Standard
      Contractual Clauses where needed (see
      <a href="https://policies.google.com/privacy/frameworks" rel="noopener" target="_blank">Google's data transfer frameworks</a>).
    </p>

    <h3>Your rights under the GDPR</h3>
    <p>
      You have the right to access, rectify and erase your personal data, to restrict or object to its
      processing, and to data portability. You also have the right to lodge a complaint with a data
      protection supervisory authority, in particular in the country where you live or work; in the UK this
      is the Information Commissioner's Office, and in Switzerland the Federal Data Protection and
      Information Commissioner. Section 12 explains how to exercise these rights.
    </p>

    <h2>12. Your rights</h2>
    <p>
      Depending on where you live, you may have rights to access, correct, delete, or port your personal
      data. Since Paperdot stores your journal only on your device, you can exercise these rights directly in
      the app: edit or delete entries, and export a backup or Markdown file to take your data elsewhere. If
      you need help, write to <a href={mailto()}>{studio.email}</a> and we will respond within 30 days.
    </p>

    <h2>13. Changes to this policy</h2>
    <p>
      If this policy changes, the updated version is published on this page with a new "updated" date.
      Paperdot may later offer optional end-to-end encrypted sync; this policy will describe it before that
      feature is released. Material changes affecting how data is shared will also be noted in the app's
      release notes.
    </p>

    <h2>14. Contact</h2>
    <p>
      Yaner Apps &mdash; <a href={mailto()}>{studio.email}</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</Layout>
